WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:06.840
MATTHEW SPALDING: Good evening.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.910
Welcome to the Washington, DC
campus of Hillsdale College.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.570
I'm Matthew Spalding.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:14.480
I'm the Kirby chair of
constitutional government.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.900
And I'm also the
dean of our programs

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.640
here in Washington, DC, where
we teach undergraduates.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.240
And soon, we'll be starting
a graduate school here

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.730
in Washington, DC, as well.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:27.380
Of course, Hillsdale
College is not

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:31.100
in Washington, DC,
[INAUDIBLE] observers of you.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.410
It's in Hillsdale, Michigan.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.950
It's an old college,
founded, if I might note,

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.520
just as a point of
reference, coincidentally,

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.640
founded the same year, 1844,
as Notre Dame was chartered

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.140
in Indiana.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:49.710
Because of Hillsdale's
unique history,

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.980
it was the first college
to prohibit its charter

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.180
getting discrimination based
on race, religion, or sex.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.816
It was an early proponent
of the abolition of slavery,

00:00:58.816 --> 00:01:03.320
and to this day,
takes no federal money

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.600
to maintain its moral,
intellectual independence.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.400
Because of all those
things, Hillsdale

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.620
has always emphasized
not only the teaching

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.040
of the liberal arts,
but the teaching

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.860
of those things necessary
for the perpetuation

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.170
of civil and religious liberty.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:20.690
And that means understanding
the principles and practices

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.700
of self-government.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.190
Indeed, as part of
our core, we require

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.070
every student of
Hillsdale College

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:31.340
to take a class on the United
States Constitution, which

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.070
brings me to our
event this evening

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:38.090
and our conversation with the
Honorable Amy Coney Barrett.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:40.340
Judge Barrett is a
graduate of Rhodes College,

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.670
with a BA in English
literature, which I point out,

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.130
as I was telling her
earlier, further evidence

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.310
that liberal arts are a
proper and good foundation

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.245
for good legal reasoning.

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:54.620
She serves on the faculty of
Notre Dame Law School, where

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.620
she earned her JD,
summa cum laude,

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.980
and was a law clerk for Judge
Laurence Silberman of the US

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.620
Court of Appeals
for the DC Circuit

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.600
and for Associate
Justice Antonin

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.840
Scalia of the US Supreme Court.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.160
From 2010 to 2016, she served
by appointment of Chief Justice

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.260
John Roberts on the
Advisory Committee

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:19.400
for the Federal Rules
of Appellate Procedure.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.070
She's written for
constitutional law

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:24.500
and statutory interpretation
in numerous journals,

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.220
law journals.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.190
And as we were
discussing earlier,

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:32.060
she still teaches one course
every semester at Notre Dame.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.060
And she was appointed to the
United States Court of Appeals

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:39.530
for the Seventh Circuit
in November of 2017.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.160
After a few prepared
remarks, she

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.520
will be interviewed this
evening by Stephanie Maloney.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.280
Stephanie is an
appellate associate

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:50.420
in the Washington, DC
office of Winston &amp; Strawn.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:55.010
She served as a law clerk
with Judge Edith Clement

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:57.830
of the United States Court of
Appeals for the Fifth Circuit

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:00.650
and Judge Stephen Murphy of the
United States District Court

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.840
for the Eastern
District of Michigan.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.170
She graduated Notre
Dame Law School,

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:11.270
where she was a student
of then-professor Barrett.

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:14.640
So with that, Judge Barrett,
welcome to Hillsdale College.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.580
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:27.680
AMY CONEY BARRETT: Thanks
so much to Dr. Spalding

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.060
for organizing this event.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.910
I am delighted to be
with you this evening.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:34.400
I am a big fan of
Hillsdale College.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.180
I've interacted with Hillsdale
faculty and students and alumni

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.850
in both social and
academic contexts.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.420
And I am very impressed
with the education

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:46.260
that Hillsdale has on offer.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.830
So I'm particularly delighted
to be with you here tonight.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:54.380
I'm going to talk this evening
about the obligations of duty

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:56.450
in the face of
private preferences,

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.730
and the obligation
of duty in the face

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:02.250
of intense public
pressure to the contrary.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:03.950
And I'm going to
begin with a story

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:06.380
from the revolutionary
war hero--

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.830
John Adams' representation
of the British soldiers who

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:11.560
had been involved in
the Boston Massacre.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.760
The Boston Massacre
happened in 1770.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.310
At that point, tensions
between the British colonists--

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.640
I'm going to move
this microphone over--

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.340
British colonists and
the Crown were rising.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:27.450
So tensions were high.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.670
And British soldiers
were stationed in Boston.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:33.210
And there was no love lost
between the British soldiers

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.400
and the Bostonians.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.470
The Boston Massacre became--

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.180
and those of you who
have been to Boston

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:43.090
and done the Freedom Trail
have seen the site of this.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:45.540
The Bostonians were
taunting the soldiers

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.190
who were stationed there.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:50.070
And what began in
taunts then turned

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:54.660
into a crowd of
300 to 400 people,

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.700
erupted into more
taunting and violence.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.700
And it ended when the British
soldiers shot into the crowd,

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.880
killing five people.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.940
The soldiers were
charged with murder.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.070
And there was little
public sympathy for them,

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:09.070
to say the least.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.620
There was a lot
of public pressure

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.260
for them to simply be lynched.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:16.530
They couldn't find
counsel to represent them.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:19.180
And then John Adams stepped up.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:21.540
Now, obviously, John
Adams was a patriot.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.540
So he couldn't have felt
a lot of personal sympathy

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:27.150
for the British soldiers.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:30.600
His sympathies were
not allied with theirs.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.550
But he believed
that it was vital

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:36.780
that the British soldiers
received a fair trial.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.990
He believed that the American
cause for self-government

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:44.520
would be harmed if Boston
justice was not truly just.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.600
And so he agreed to
represent the soldiers.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.180
Yet he was reviled by many
Bostonians for doing so.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:54.930
He faced intense public
pressure about that decision,

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.910
about his choice to
represent the enemy.

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:01.410
And here's what Adams
himself had to say about it.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.820
"I had devoted myself to
endless labor and anxiety,

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:07.830
if not to infamy and
to death, and that

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:11.760
for nothing except what
was and ought to be,

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.610
all in all, a sense of duty."

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:18.430
John Adams put aside
personal preferences.

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.920
He stood up to public pressure.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:22.840
And he did his duty.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:25.210
And moreover, he did it to
the best of his ability.

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:28.270
He didn't do his
grudging, minimal best.

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:29.658
He did his absolute best.

00:06:29.658 --> 00:06:32.200
And the captain was acquitted,
and most of the soldiers were.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.760
And those who were not
escaped the death penalty.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.910
Now, John Adams did
these things as a lawyer.

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:43.380
But adhering to duty in the
face of a contrary personal or

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.530
political preference and in
the face of public pressure

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.740
to the contrary has
particular resonance

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:52.670
for the job of a judge.

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:57.260
A judge is obligated to
apply the law as it is,

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:59.960
and not as she
wishes it would be.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:02.600
She is obliged to
follow the law,

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:06.260
even when her personal
preferences cut the other way

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:08.930
or when she will experience
great public criticism

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:11.130
for doing so.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:12.890
I'm going to talk
about this with a focus

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:14.840
on the Supreme
Court, because those

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:17.930
are the cases with which you
will be the most familiar

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:21.450
and the judges who
you'll be familiar with.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.900
We have been fortunate
in our history

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:27.920
to have justices who have
been willing to do their duty,

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.560
even when it may run against
their personal sympathies,

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:34.590
and even when it will subject
them to public criticism.

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.640
I'm going to give you an
example from a famous dissent--

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.950
the dissent of
Justice Robert Jackson

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:44.070
in the case of Korematsu
versus the United States.

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:47.550
That case challenged the
constitutionality of the order

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:50.970
commanding the exclusion of
the Japanese from certain areas

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:53.840
during World War II.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:58.880
The imposition of curfews,
exclusions, and ultimately

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:05.010
the Japanese internment is
a stain on American history.

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:08.220
As a congressional commission
later described it,

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:13.180
the internment was motivated by
racial prejudice, war hysteria,

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:16.410
and a failure of
political leadership.

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:18.820
But the widespread
public support

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:21.400
for the Japanese internment
is reflected in the fact

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.610
that Earl Warren, who was
then governor of California

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:27.490
and later became our chief
justice, who led the court when

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:29.650
Brown versus the Board
of Education was decided,

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:31.810
supported the
Japanese internment.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:35.590
It was a time of war and
people were carried away.

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:39.490
Now, the internment of the
Japanese derived its authority

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:42.730
from an executive order that had
been signed by Franklin Delano

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:48.820
Roosevelt. Robert Jackson
owed his career to Roosevelt.

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:51.310
Roosevelt had appointed
him to the three

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:54.670
jobs that are the most
prestigious that any lawyer can

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:55.610
have.

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:57.610
Robert Jackson appointed
him to be the solicitor

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:00.100
general of the United
States, the attorney

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:02.260
general of the United
States, and he appointed him

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:05.580
to his seat on
the Supreme Court.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.990
And in addition to their
professional relationship,

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:10.000
they were friends--

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:10.760
longtime friends.

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:13.110
They were both from New York.

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:15.720
Yet Jackson did
not allow whatever

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:20.820
personal loyalty or affection
that he had for Roosevelt

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:23.390
influence his
decision in that case.

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:25.110
Unfortunately, he
was in the minority.

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:26.250
His was a dissent.

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:29.880
The Court decided 6-3
that the exclusion order

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:31.830
was constitutional.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:34.590
But Jackson wrote
a vehement dissent,

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:36.690
objecting to the
constitutionality

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:41.040
of an order that rounded
up people, many of them,

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.310
most of them American
citizens, simply because they

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.620
were of Japanese descent.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:49.020
He argued that that
could not be justified

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:51.990
as an exercise of
military power.

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:54.150
There had been no
suspension of habeas corpus.

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:56.610
And that is the provision
in the Constitution

00:09:56.610 --> 00:10:01.540
that permits the exercise of
that kind of emergency power.

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:04.830
Duty, not politics.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:08.220
Being a judge takes courage--

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:11.100
the courage of Robert Jackson.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:16.230
You are not there to decide
cases as you may prefer.

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:19.360
You are not there to
decide cases as the public

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:22.360
or as the press may want you to.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:25.580
You're not there to win
a popularity contest.

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:29.440
You are there to do your duty
and to follow the law wherever

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.290
it may take you.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:33.600
Justice Scalia used to
say that a judge who

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:36.000
likes every result
that she reaches

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.230
is not a very good judge.

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:40.730
In fact, she's a very bad judge.

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:43.670
The law simply does not
align with a judge's

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:48.360
political preference or personal
preference in every case.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:50.600
And so it will be
the case that judges

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:54.890
have to make hard decisions and
that they have to decide cases

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.930
in ways that yield
outcomes that are not

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:59.120
the outcomes they would prefer.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:01.780
I've been at this only for
about a year and a half.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:04.460
And I can attest that that
has happened to me many times

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:06.050
already.

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:07.610
But that is the
duty of a judge--

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:10.910
to decide cases in the
way that the law requires

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:15.200
them to be decided, and
not simply from the hip.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.390
If I'm saying, what
would Amy Barrett

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:19.520
like to see the result
in this case be?

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:26.090
If the judge is willing
not to apply the law

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:30.090
but to decide cases in
a line, in accordance

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:33.240
with personal preference
rather than the law,

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:36.330
then she's not actually
functioning as a judge at all.

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:39.720
She's functioning
as a policymaker.

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:45.030
The fact that every judge
wears a nondescript black robe,

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:47.340
I think, is a powerful
testament to this fact.

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:52.650
It symbolizes the impersonality
of the law and the dedication

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:57.370
that each judge should
have to the rule of law.

00:11:57.370 --> 00:11:59.500
The black robe
was not the custom

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:02.290
of the nation at its beginning.

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:04.820
At the start, judges--

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:07.390
including federal judges,
including the first justices

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:09.070
of the Supreme Court--

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:11.080
wore colored robes.

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:14.650
They would wear red robes
trimmed in ermine in the custom

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:15.790
of British judges.

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:19.000
Or some of them
wore robes that were

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:21.310
in the colors of the
academic institution

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:23.500
from which they had
graduated, so much

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:26.290
like academics when they
wear regalia at commencement.

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:29.700
So that was the custom
of judges at the time.

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:32.760
But John Marshall, who was
not our first chief justice

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.710
but was the foundational
and probably

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:36.990
the greatest of chief
justices, is the one

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:39.590
who changed that tradition.

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:42.510
At John Marshall's
investiture, he showed up

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:45.380
wearing a simple black robe.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:48.800
And soon thereafter, his
brethren, the other members

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:51.260
of the Court, followed suit.

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:55.920
And today, all federal judges
wear a simple black robe

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:59.760
in deciding cases when they sit.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:01.230
So why do I think
that's important?

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:04.970
Why do I think that reflects
the symbol of the impersonality

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:07.150
of the law?

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:11.350
Because the focus should not
be who is deciding the case.

00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:13.120
The focus shouldn't
be on the judge.

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:15.190
The judge shouldn't
be drawing attention

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:17.810
to herself or himself.

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:20.900
The focus should be on the law.

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:22.720
The judge should be
deciding the case

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:24.520
not in accordance
with her personal,

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:26.180
with her individual preferences.

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:31.010
So the black robe doesn't
express individuality.

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:34.040
And a judge should not be
deciding cases in accordance

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:37.130
with her individual preferences.

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:43.100
The black robe symbolizes that
all judges share a dedication

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:45.830
to the rule of law,
that we are engaged

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:49.700
in a common enterprise
of applying the law

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:53.010
and doing our best job at it.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:55.530
Justice should not turn
on what judge you get.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:58.860
It should turn on
what the law requires.

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:01.080
Now, you might be saying
to yourselves, well, that's

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:02.190
all and good.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:04.386
But judges are human.

00:14:04.386 --> 00:14:06.870
Human beings have preferences.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:09.690
Human beings have commitments.

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:14.010
And how could human
beings ever separate out

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:20.240
what their personal preferences
are from what the law requires?

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:23.650
I insist that that is possible.

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:27.300
It is something that every
judge must be vigilant about.

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:28.350
But it isn't possible.

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:30.190
That is the nature of the job.

00:14:30.190 --> 00:14:32.243
And that is the judicial duty.

00:14:32.243 --> 00:14:33.662
[CLAPPING]

00:14:33.662 --> 00:14:35.554
[LAUGHING]

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:41.540
I'm going to share with you
a couple of my own techniques

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:46.020
that I use to try to guard
against any sort of bias.

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:48.810
When I write an opinion,
I try to view it

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:51.990
through the eyes of the party
that I'm ruling against,

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:53.850
through the losing party.

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:56.925
So I try to imagine that
it is me or someone I love.

00:14:56.925 --> 00:14:58.800
Let's imagine it's a
criminal case, that it's

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:02.340
one of my own children
who is on the other side,

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:04.740
to try to check
and make sure, am I

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:07.290
deciding this case in
the most honest way,

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:10.710
and not out of any personal
bias or personal sympathy?

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:13.800
If this was someone who I
loved who was on the line,

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:17.310
could I honestly say that
this reasoning-- obviously I

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:20.280
would want to decide the case
in favor of my child, right?

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:24.390
But could I honestly say
that this reasoning is one

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:28.080
that I would apply to
someone that I loved dearly?

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:30.030
And I do the same
thing for colleagues

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:32.290
that might disagree with me.

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:35.740
So the job of judges is
to sift through arguments.

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:37.562
Sometimes the
arguments in a case,

00:15:37.562 --> 00:15:39.270
especially at the
Court of Appeals level,

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:40.440
will be pretty one-sided.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.030
And there'll be
pretty easy agreement

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:45.850
among the three-judge panel
about what the law requires.

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:47.880
But the cases are
sometimes closer,

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:51.330
and there will be good
arguments on either side.

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:56.070
And it's the job of the judge
to decide which of the arguments

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.020
is better.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:01.670
And as I draft an opinion
in that kind of case,

00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:04.310
I do it with an eye towards
what colleagues who are likely

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:05.630
to disagree with me.

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:08.120
What would the dissent think?

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:10.100
Do my arguments hold up?

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:12.230
Have I really given due
weight to everything

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:13.040
on the other side?

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:16.060
What will the dissent
say about this?

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:17.840
And if I write the
opinion that way,

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:20.300
I am really giving the
arguments on the other side

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:22.490
their full due.

00:16:22.490 --> 00:16:25.820
Because when a judge has tunnel
vision, when a judge makes

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:29.300
a knee-jerk decision about how
a case should be decided based

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:32.090
on how the judge wants
to see it decided,

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:33.980
then the judge has
a hard time guarding

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:38.270
against that risk of personal
preferences creeping in.

00:16:38.270 --> 00:16:40.550
It's only when the judge
disciplines herself

00:16:40.550 --> 00:16:44.360
to really consider the best
legal arguments on both sides

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:49.820
of a question that the judge
can do her best job at trying

00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:53.780
to make sure she is adhering to
that duty of following the law

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:58.170
wherever the law may lead.

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:00.810
Our foundational
constitutional commitment

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:03.430
is to the rule of law.

00:17:03.430 --> 00:17:06.790
And for more than two
centuries, the federal judiciary

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:09.609
has worked assiduously, if
not always successfully,

00:17:09.609 --> 00:17:12.790
has worked very hard
and mostly successfully

00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:15.500
to develop and uphold
the rule of law.

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:17.260
We have an amazing Constitution.

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:20.079
And we have an amazing
judicial system.

00:17:20.079 --> 00:17:21.490
We should be proud of it.

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:24.510
And we want it to continue.

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:26.880
For my own part,
I became a judge

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:28.650
to contribute to that tradition.

00:17:28.650 --> 00:17:30.570
And I would have had
no interest in the job

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:32.820
if the job was
about policymaking

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:34.440
and about making
policy decisions.

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:38.580
My interest is in contributing
to our tradition of judges

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:41.732
upholding the rule of law.

00:17:41.732 --> 00:17:43.940
And there's a lot of talk
these days about the courts

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:46.770
being mere political
institutions.

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:49.738
But if we reduce the
courts to mere politics,

00:17:49.738 --> 00:17:50.780
then why do we need them?

00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:53.120
We already have politicians.

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:56.330
Courts are not
arenas for politics.

00:17:56.330 --> 00:18:00.350
Courts are places where
judges discharge the duty

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:03.087
to uphold the rule of law.

00:18:03.087 --> 00:18:05.670
I'm going to stop there and then
invite my student, Stephanie,

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:06.570
to come up.

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:08.883
And we'll just have a little
bit of back and forth.

00:18:08.883 --> 00:18:12.264
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:19.030 --> 00:18:21.070
STEPHANIE MALONEY:
So to start, I'd

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:23.483
like to talk with you about
the confirmation process.

00:18:23.483 --> 00:18:24.400
AMY CONEY BARRETT: OK.

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:26.150
STEPHANIE MALONEY: It
wasn't that long ago

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:27.370
that you went through it.

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:30.400
And there was a lot
of media discussion

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:33.250
about whether the faith
of a judicial nominee

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:35.500
is relevant in the
confirmation process.

00:18:35.500 --> 00:18:39.950
You yourself got quite a bit
of attention on that question.

00:18:39.950 --> 00:18:44.200
So what role, if any, should
faith of a nominee have

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:45.860
in the confirmation process?

00:18:45.860 --> 00:18:46.860
AMY CONEY BARRETT: None.

00:18:48.783 --> 00:18:49.950
STEPHANIE MALONEY: Say more.

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:51.220
That's not enough.

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:52.220
AMY CONEY BARRETT: Yeah.

00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:54.690
I wouldn't have let you get
away with a one-word answer.

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:55.240
None.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:57.940
I mean, we have a long
tradition of religious tolerance

00:18:57.940 --> 00:18:58.970
in this country.

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:02.260
And in fact, the religious
test clause in the Constitution

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:06.220
makes it unconstitutional
to impose a religious test

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:09.170
on anyone who holds
public office.

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:13.480
So whether someone is Catholic
or Jewish or Evangelical or

00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:17.090
Muslim or has no faith at
all is irrelevant to the job.

00:19:17.090 --> 00:19:19.720
And in fact, it's
unconstitutional to consider it

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:21.718
as a qualification for office.

00:19:21.718 --> 00:19:24.010
I think there's been a lot
of public debate about that.

00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:25.870
So I don't think I
have much more to add.

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:28.030
I think that that's just clear.

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:32.270
I do have one thing that I
want to add to that, though.

00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:33.700
I think when you
step back and you

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:39.100
think about the debate about
whether someone's religion has

00:19:39.100 --> 00:19:42.940
any bearing on their
fitness for office,

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:45.600
it seems to me that the
premise of the question

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:49.470
is that people of faith would
have a uniquely difficult time

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:52.050
separating out their
moral commitments

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:55.600
from their obligation
to apply the law.

00:19:55.600 --> 00:20:00.270
And I think people of faith
should reject that premise.

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:03.840
All people, of course--
well, we hope, most people--

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:06.460
have deeply held
moral convictions,

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:08.340
whether or not they
come from faith.

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:11.370
People who have no faith,
people who are not religious,

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:14.400
have deeply held
moral convictions.

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:19.530
And it's just as important
for those people to be sure--

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:23.310
I just spent time talking
about the job of a judge being

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:26.790
to set aside moral convictions,
personal moral convictions

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:29.430
and personal preferences,
and follow the law.

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:31.440
That's a challenge
for those of faith

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:33.670
and for those who have no faith.

00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:37.350
So I think the public should
be absolutely concerned about

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:40.110
whether a nominee
for judicial office

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:45.030
will be willing and able to
set aside personal preferences,

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:47.580
be they moral, be
they political,

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:49.110
whatever convictions they are.

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:52.590
The public should be concerned
about whether a nominee can

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:55.480
set those aside in favor
of following the law.

00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:57.720
But that's not a challenge
just for religious people.

00:20:57.720 --> 00:20:59.500
I mean, that's a
challenge for everyone.

00:20:59.500 --> 00:21:01.320
And so I think it's
a dangerous road

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:04.470
to go down to say that only
religious people would not

00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:10.740
be able to separate out moral
convictions from their duty.

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:15.330
STEPHANIE MALONEY: So in
the same way that you just

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:17.580
answered-- we separate
faith from the law.

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:20.650
You talked about how we
separate politics from the law.

00:21:20.650 --> 00:21:24.120
So how does that map onto the
number of five-four decisions

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:26.790
in the Court, which
seem to often break down

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:28.100
along partisan lines?

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:32.283
Would you say that the Court
is becoming more politicized?

00:21:32.283 --> 00:21:33.700
AMY CONEY BARRETT:
No, I wouldn't.

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:37.340
And that's obviously a
popular narrative right now.

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:41.650
And it has been at
other points in history.

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:45.070
As I tell my students,
when in my former life

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:47.260
as a constitutional
law professor,

00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:49.240
I think that those
differences are not

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:52.400
about partisan politics.

00:21:52.400 --> 00:21:57.100
I don't know any judges
who come to the bench

00:21:57.100 --> 00:21:59.140
with the aim of
saying, I am going

00:21:59.140 --> 00:22:01.960
to bring into
effect the platform

00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:05.800
of the party with maybe
which I was once affiliated.

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:07.930
That's not true of those
who have been nominated

00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:09.093
by Democratic presidents.

00:22:09.093 --> 00:22:11.260
And that's not true of those
who have been nominated

00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:13.850
by Republican presidents.

00:22:13.850 --> 00:22:18.190
So I don't think that five-four
decisions or splits on courts

00:22:18.190 --> 00:22:21.820
are explicable by
partisan commitments

00:22:21.820 --> 00:22:24.610
or by outcomes in
particular cases.

00:22:24.610 --> 00:22:27.610
I think they're explicable
by starting points,

00:22:27.610 --> 00:22:29.590
by first-order commitments.

00:22:29.590 --> 00:22:32.200
So there are differences
in ways that judges

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:35.530
approach the enterprise of
interpreting the Constitution.

00:22:35.530 --> 00:22:38.110
I'll give you a
couple of examples.

00:22:38.110 --> 00:22:40.780
All judges think that
the original meaning

00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:42.280
of the Constitution--

00:22:42.280 --> 00:22:44.860
its history, the way
that it was understood

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:47.770
by those who ratified
it, who drafted it,

00:22:47.770 --> 00:22:49.750
the founding generation--

00:22:49.750 --> 00:22:54.520
all judges take that as a
data point, as relevant.

00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:57.390
Those who are committed
to originalism

00:22:57.390 --> 00:23:00.400
treat it as determinative
when the original meaning

00:23:00.400 --> 00:23:02.170
is discernible.

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:04.360
Others just treat
it as a data point,

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:07.100
but one that would not
necessarily control.

00:23:07.100 --> 00:23:10.060
So that will yield different
outcomes in different cases.

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:12.430
It's the same thing with
constitutional text.

00:23:12.430 --> 00:23:15.610
Some judges take an approach
to the text that says, listen.

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:18.490
If the text is clear,
that's non-negotiable.

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:20.998
Others take an
approach to the text

00:23:20.998 --> 00:23:22.540
that treats it as
having a little bit

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:24.940
more play in the joints.

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:25.470
OK?

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:29.250
Some judges approach
the Constitution saying,

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:31.985
there are some
constitutional commitments

00:23:31.985 --> 00:23:33.360
that we're not
going to back down

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:36.730
from because the
Constitution enshrines them.

00:23:36.730 --> 00:23:39.330
But with respect to those
that the Constitution does not

00:23:39.330 --> 00:23:42.420
speak, we're going to leave
it to democratic majorities

00:23:42.420 --> 00:23:44.010
to work out.

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:46.530
Others see the
Constitution as having

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:48.660
a more amorphous
and evolving content

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:53.580
and speaking to evolving
values and majority--

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:57.840
evolving values in ways that
then democratic majorities

00:23:57.840 --> 00:24:00.090
don't have the freedom
to make choices.

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:01.590
So they're just
different approaches

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:03.240
to constitutional
interpretation that

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:07.880
will yield different results
in many hot-button cases.

00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:09.520
But that's a lot
different than saying,

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:11.220
well, it's because
such and such a judge

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:14.953
goes in and has these
particular five commitments that

00:24:14.953 --> 00:24:16.620
are consistent with
the Democratic Party

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:19.090
platform or the
Republican Party platform.

00:24:19.090 --> 00:24:22.500
So it's really a difference
in one's understanding

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:24.867
of the Constitution and
of the role of a judge.

00:24:24.867 --> 00:24:26.700
STEPHANIE MALONEY: So
you spoke a little bit

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:30.690
about the way in which you
approach opinion writing, how

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:33.540
you separate your
personal preferences

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:36.510
and take an objective view.

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:39.270
When President Obama
nominated Justice Sotomayor

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:41.700
to the Supreme Court,
he famously said

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:47.400
that judges should be shaped by
empathy as much as by reason,

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:51.270
and that, quote, "a common
touch of compassion," end quote,

00:24:51.270 --> 00:24:55.980
is a necessary ingredient
in a justice or a judge.

00:24:55.980 --> 00:25:00.090
So do you think empathy or
compassion have a role to play?

00:25:00.090 --> 00:25:02.460
Do they have a role to
play in legal reasoning

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:04.653
and legal writing?

00:25:04.653 --> 00:25:06.320
AMY CONEY BARRETT:
Well, I think empathy

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:08.120
and compassion
have a role to play

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:10.040
in the life of every
human being, right?

00:25:10.040 --> 00:25:12.530
I mean, we should
all be empathetic,

00:25:12.530 --> 00:25:14.630
and we should all
be compassionate.

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:16.670
And as people, I
think that's something

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:18.650
that we want to encourage.

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:20.720
Do I think it has a
role in legal reasoning?

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:23.720
I would say no.

00:25:23.720 --> 00:25:25.340
I would say yes,
in this respect.

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:28.100
I don't think-- and I try when
I draft opinions to have this

00:25:28.100 --> 00:25:29.580
in mind--

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:31.790
I don't think judges
should ever be harsh.

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:33.170
So I don't think--

00:25:33.170 --> 00:25:35.450
I think you should
be compassionate

00:25:35.450 --> 00:25:37.340
to the losing party
who might find it

00:25:37.340 --> 00:25:38.660
difficult to read the opinion.

00:25:38.660 --> 00:25:40.640
I'm aware that the
party I'm ruling against

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:41.700
will read the opinion.

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:45.660
And so I want it to
be not worded harshly,

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:50.120
to be phrased in a way
that's kind and easy to take.

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:54.440
But should the law be driven
by compassion or empathy?

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:56.090
And I would say
the answer to that

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:58.490
is no, because
that gets mixed up

00:25:58.490 --> 00:26:02.670
in the judge's personal
preferences or feelings.

00:26:02.670 --> 00:26:04.620
So I think when the
law requires a result,

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:08.210
sometimes the law requires
results that are hard.

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:12.770
I think sometimes that the
penalties or the sentences

00:26:12.770 --> 00:26:15.210
that statutes impose
for particular crimes--

00:26:15.210 --> 00:26:17.833
I think this would be something
that district judges face

00:26:17.833 --> 00:26:20.000
all the time when they
sentence criminal defendants.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:23.810
Sometimes they don't have
a lot of discretion because

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:27.530
of the system that the statute
sets, in terms of how long

00:26:27.530 --> 00:26:29.270
the sentence must be.

00:26:29.270 --> 00:26:31.870
And that might be very hard for
them to impose that sentence.

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:33.710
But if that's what
the law requires,

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:36.620
you can't have judges
saying, that's not

00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:39.360
what I'm going to do because
my heart tells me otherwise.

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:43.400
So I think what we want
in judges, again, is

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:44.810
judges who will follow the law.

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:48.350
And we want to be
compassionate people.

00:26:48.350 --> 00:26:51.720
We certainly want to be aware
of the effect of our decisions.

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:54.380
But I think you get
into a dangerous place

00:26:54.380 --> 00:26:57.350
when you say that you're going
to follow your heart and not

00:26:57.350 --> 00:27:00.170
your head in deciding a case.

00:27:00.170 --> 00:27:02.930
STEPHANIE MALONEY: You've talked
about, at least in conversation

00:27:02.930 --> 00:27:07.000
with me, about how frequently
the Seventh Circuit writes

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:07.970
opinions.

00:27:07.970 --> 00:27:12.560
And you yourself have written
quite a few in criminal cases.

00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:17.450
Is that something that has
informed your approach,

00:27:17.450 --> 00:27:21.620
in terms of paying attention to
the language that you're using

00:27:21.620 --> 00:27:25.270
and whether you're coming across
as harsh in those opinions?

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:26.270
AMY CONEY BARRETT: Sure.

00:27:26.270 --> 00:27:28.520
I try to be conscious
when I write the opinions

00:27:28.520 --> 00:27:31.220
that the people who I'm
writing that-- let's

00:27:31.220 --> 00:27:33.740
imagine that I'm
affirming a conviction

00:27:33.740 --> 00:27:35.700
or affirming a sentence.

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:39.410
If it were my child whose
conviction was being affirmed

00:27:39.410 --> 00:27:41.060
or whose sentence
was being affirmed,

00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:43.490
I would want the opinion
written in a very respectful

00:27:43.490 --> 00:27:46.670
and well-reasoned way,
and not in a snide way

00:27:46.670 --> 00:27:50.792
or to use it as an opportunity
to get some cute lines in

00:27:50.792 --> 00:27:51.750
or something like that.

00:27:51.750 --> 00:27:52.980
So yeah.

00:27:52.980 --> 00:27:54.590
I think it's very
important to realize

00:27:54.590 --> 00:27:56.090
this is serious
business that you're

00:27:56.090 --> 00:27:58.650
entrusted with, heavy
responsibility as a judge.

00:27:58.650 --> 00:28:01.320
And so you should write
your opinions accordingly.

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:05.060
This is real stuff, and
people's liberty on the line.

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:07.460
STEPHANIE MALONEY: You've
had one opinion that

00:28:07.460 --> 00:28:08.925
drew some-- many
opinions that drew

00:28:08.925 --> 00:28:11.300
attention, one in particular
that I'd like to talk about.

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:14.480
You wrote a dissent in
Kanter versus Barr, which

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:16.550
was a Second Amendment case.

00:28:16.550 --> 00:28:19.910
And in your dissent, you
studied founding era documents.

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:22.250
Can you talk a little
bit about the background

00:28:22.250 --> 00:28:25.440
of the case for those of us
who are unfamiliar with it?

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:27.170
And then talk
about your approach

00:28:27.170 --> 00:28:28.870
to those large
constitutional questions.

00:28:28.870 --> 00:28:29.870
AMY CONEY BARRETT: Sure.

00:28:29.870 --> 00:28:36.690
So Kanter versus Barr was a case
in which the defendant-- well,

00:28:36.690 --> 00:28:39.470
he had been convicted
of Medicaid fraud.

00:28:39.470 --> 00:28:44.540
He had sold shoe inserts
that didn't actually

00:28:44.540 --> 00:28:47.470
comply with the right
standards and then

00:28:47.470 --> 00:28:50.930
been refunded, gotten
money for them anyway.

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:51.558
It was fraud.

00:28:51.558 --> 00:28:52.850
And he had served his sentence.

00:28:52.850 --> 00:28:55.670
But under federal
law, those who've

00:28:55.670 --> 00:28:57.800
been convicted of a
felony, of any felony,

00:28:57.800 --> 00:28:59.540
lose their gun
rights, so that they

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:02.430
can't possess a gun thereafter.

00:29:02.430 --> 00:29:06.440
So Kanter argued that
he wasn't a threat.

00:29:06.440 --> 00:29:07.650
This was his only conviction.

00:29:07.650 --> 00:29:10.070
It wasn't a violent crime.

00:29:10.070 --> 00:29:11.810
He had discharged his sentence.

00:29:11.810 --> 00:29:14.000
He didn't use
illegal substances.

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:14.720
He was stable.

00:29:14.720 --> 00:29:15.410
He had a job.

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:16.130
He was married.

00:29:16.130 --> 00:29:19.590
And time had passed
since this conviction.

00:29:19.590 --> 00:29:21.470
And he was not a recidivist.

00:29:21.470 --> 00:29:24.530
So he argued that it was
unconstitutional to deprive

00:29:24.530 --> 00:29:26.570
him of his Second
Amendment right

00:29:26.570 --> 00:29:31.610
to possess a firearm because
he did not pose any danger.

00:29:31.610 --> 00:29:36.110
And the three-judge panel, two
of my colleagues rejected that

00:29:36.110 --> 00:29:39.920
claim and said that the Second
Amendment doesn't preclude--

00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:43.280
in Kanter's case, there was both
a state statute from Wisconsin

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:47.330
and then a federal statute
that made it illegal for felons

00:29:47.330 --> 00:29:49.430
to possess guns.

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:53.850
So the majority said that
that was constitutional.

00:29:53.850 --> 00:29:57.390
And I wrote a dissent saying
that it was unconstitutional.

00:29:57.390 --> 00:29:59.480
It's unusual, at the
Court of Appeals level,

00:29:59.480 --> 00:30:02.390
to encounter a
case which requires

00:30:02.390 --> 00:30:06.070
a deep dive into
founding era documents,

00:30:06.070 --> 00:30:08.020
because at the Court
of Appeals level,

00:30:08.020 --> 00:30:10.960
so many cases have already been
decided by the Supreme Court

00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:12.920
that you're typically
applying precedent.

00:30:12.920 --> 00:30:14.770
You're applying Supreme
Court precedent.

00:30:14.770 --> 00:30:17.020
The Second Amendment
is newer territory.

00:30:17.020 --> 00:30:20.920
The Supreme Court decided Heller
versus United States just--

00:30:20.920 --> 00:30:24.500
Heller versus District of
Columbia-- not that long ago.

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:27.250
And it's only taken one
other Second Amendment case

00:30:27.250 --> 00:30:27.800
since then.

00:30:27.800 --> 00:30:30.760
So there's just not a lot
of precedent out there.

00:30:30.760 --> 00:30:34.930
So I had to do a pretty
deep dive into the history

00:30:34.930 --> 00:30:36.610
of the Second Amendment.

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:39.670
And Heller, the
Supreme Court case,

00:30:39.670 --> 00:30:42.340
is pretty clear about the fact
that the scope of the Second

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:46.800
Amendment needs to be
determined by its history.

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:50.700
And I concluded, after
serving all of the arguments--

00:30:50.700 --> 00:30:54.090
the historically-based arguments
for the constitutionality

00:30:54.090 --> 00:30:58.620
of depriving felons of their
right to possess firearms--

00:30:58.620 --> 00:31:01.290
that there was no
blanket authorization

00:31:01.290 --> 00:31:04.350
to just take guns, that the
right to possess a gun away

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:07.290
from someone who
committed a felony.

00:31:07.290 --> 00:31:11.080
That sounds kind of radical, to
say felons can have firearms.

00:31:11.080 --> 00:31:14.970
But I think it's because what
the longstanding prohibitions

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:19.020
were, and in fact, had
been, even under federal law

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:21.900
until more recently, was
that violent felons couldn't

00:31:21.900 --> 00:31:23.340
have firearms.

00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:26.250
And there's a longstanding--
what the history showed me was

00:31:26.250 --> 00:31:30.510
that there's been a longstanding
practice of saying that those

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:35.460
who pose a threat of violence
to the community cannot have

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:36.000
firearms.

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:37.167
And that makes sense, right?

00:31:37.167 --> 00:31:40.140
History is consistent
with commonsense.

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:43.230
Those who would be
risky with guns,

00:31:43.230 --> 00:31:46.950
who would pose danger
with guns, then the state

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:48.940
can take guns away.

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:52.230
But in the instance of
someone who the state has not

00:31:52.230 --> 00:31:55.200
shown that the person
has demonstrated

00:31:55.200 --> 00:32:00.420
any risk, the mere status of
having committed a felony,

00:32:00.420 --> 00:32:03.570
I found no historical
support for saying

00:32:03.570 --> 00:32:06.690
that the state was then
justified in taking it away.

00:32:06.690 --> 00:32:10.110
So as I said, in that opinion,
if the state had evidence

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:14.490
that Mr. Kanter would have
posed some threat of violence

00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:17.800
if he'd had a gun, then that
would be a different situation.

00:32:17.800 --> 00:32:20.820
But the mere fact that
someone had committed a felony

00:32:20.820 --> 00:32:21.600
wasn't enough.

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:24.360
And you know,
over-criminalization

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:26.980
gets a lot of talk these days.

00:32:26.980 --> 00:32:31.050
And when you look at the number
of things that are felonies,

00:32:31.050 --> 00:32:31.890
you can--

00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:35.173
it's a felony-- now I'm
kind of talking out the hip.

00:32:35.173 --> 00:32:37.590
And I'll see if I can remember
exactly which state it was.

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:39.507
I think it was Massachusetts
that made selling

00:32:39.507 --> 00:32:41.730
pigs without a license--

00:32:41.730 --> 00:32:45.660
returning out-of-state bottles
for deposits in Michigan

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:48.100
can be a felony when it's
over a certain amount.

00:32:48.100 --> 00:32:50.790
So there's not really a
limit on what states can

00:32:50.790 --> 00:32:53.400
choose to define as felonies.

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:57.380
And so it seems a
little bit odd to say

00:32:57.380 --> 00:32:59.870
that the scope of one's
Second Amendment right

00:32:59.870 --> 00:33:03.500
would depend on what a state
chooses to define as a felony

00:33:03.500 --> 00:33:04.550
or not.

00:33:04.550 --> 00:33:06.260
So I concluded that
the line really

00:33:06.260 --> 00:33:09.150
isn't whether someone
is a felon or not.

00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:12.470
But the line is really whether
someone is dangerous or not.

00:33:12.470 --> 00:33:14.200
Dangerous people
shouldn't have guns.

00:33:14.200 --> 00:33:17.870
But simply saying that
someone has committed a felony

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:20.630
isn't a good enough
reason, without markers

00:33:20.630 --> 00:33:21.680
of dangerousness.

00:33:21.680 --> 00:33:22.410
But it was hard.

00:33:22.410 --> 00:33:26.870
I mean, at the Supreme Court,
when I clerked, it's even,

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:29.720
I think, become
truer and truer, you

00:33:29.720 --> 00:33:32.360
have so many briefs from amici.

00:33:32.360 --> 00:33:34.370
So friends of the
court will file briefs.

00:33:34.370 --> 00:33:36.650
And they will give you all
this historical research.

00:33:36.650 --> 00:33:39.650
And you have
excellent advocates.

00:33:39.650 --> 00:33:42.470
And so the Court has less
groundwork of its own

00:33:42.470 --> 00:33:45.760
to do because the
briefing is so rich.

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:48.710
And the same is not true
at the Court of Appeals.

00:33:48.710 --> 00:33:51.580
You just don't have all these
amicus briefs coming in.

00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:55.380
So my law clerks and I had to
do a lot of research on our own.

00:33:55.380 --> 00:33:56.722
So it was a heavy lift.

00:33:56.722 --> 00:33:59.180
STEPHANIE MALONEY: I bet there
are some upcoming law review

00:33:59.180 --> 00:34:02.700
articles about
them, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:06.020
So if you're looking at
founding era documents,

00:34:06.020 --> 00:34:08.780
would you describe
yourself as an originalist?

00:34:08.780 --> 00:34:12.690
And if so, could you describe
your view of originalism,

00:34:12.690 --> 00:34:14.250
what that means to you?

00:34:14.250 --> 00:34:15.250
AMY CONEY BARRETT: Yeah.

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:18.260
Yeah, I do consider myself
to be an originalist.

00:34:18.260 --> 00:34:19.969
And my view of
originalism, you know,

00:34:19.969 --> 00:34:24.239
originalists have two
basic commitments.

00:34:24.239 --> 00:34:28.370
One is that the meaning of the
law is fixed as of the time

00:34:28.370 --> 00:34:32.420
that it's ratified, and then
that the original meaning

00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:36.150
of that law controls
if it's discernible.

00:34:36.150 --> 00:34:40.670
So sometimes laws will have more
open-ended-- or constitutional

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:43.370
provisions, even-- might use
more open-ended language,

00:34:43.370 --> 00:34:46.520
like the word reasonableness
in the Fourth Amendment.

00:34:46.520 --> 00:34:49.909
Sometimes the language of
a constitutional provision

00:34:49.909 --> 00:34:50.699
is clear.

00:34:50.699 --> 00:34:54.710
And if the law is
clear, an originalist

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:57.860
would say that the judge
doesn't have the discretion

00:34:57.860 --> 00:34:59.700
to depart from it.

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:03.330
So I do consider
myself originalist.

00:35:03.330 --> 00:35:08.210
And I think when I
was in law school,

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:11.180
I think I started gravitating
towards that as a first year

00:35:11.180 --> 00:35:13.400
when I took constitutional
criminal procedure.

00:35:13.400 --> 00:35:16.440
And my professor was
not an originalist.

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:20.220
But that was my first exposure
to constitutional cases.

00:35:20.220 --> 00:35:21.980
And I didn't know--

00:35:21.980 --> 00:35:23.570
I didn't go to
Hillsdale College.

00:35:23.570 --> 00:35:29.010
So I had not studied
originalism as an undergraduate.

00:35:29.010 --> 00:35:32.060
So I wasn't familiar
when I entered law school

00:35:32.060 --> 00:35:34.190
with originalism as a theory.

00:35:34.190 --> 00:35:39.230
But I found myself, as I
read more and more cases,

00:35:39.230 --> 00:35:42.530
becoming more and more convinced
that the opinions that I

00:35:42.530 --> 00:35:46.280
read that took the originalist
approach were right.

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:48.680
And then as I progressed
through my studies

00:35:48.680 --> 00:35:50.600
and then did more
theoretical work,

00:35:50.600 --> 00:35:52.160
I became even more
convinced of that,

00:35:52.160 --> 00:35:54.680
because that's where
the Constitution gets

00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:55.790
its authority, right?

00:35:55.790 --> 00:35:58.880
I mean, we agree to
live by the Constitution

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:01.250
until it's lawfully changed.

00:36:01.250 --> 00:36:03.570
And judges can't change it.

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:05.100
That's not democratic, right?

00:36:05.100 --> 00:36:09.360
We change it by the prescribed
process, which is Article V.

00:36:09.360 --> 00:36:12.560
Now, the Constitution doesn't
answer every question.

00:36:12.560 --> 00:36:15.360
And it gives a lot
of flexibility.

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:18.320
It gives that flexibility
to democratic majorities

00:36:18.320 --> 00:36:23.210
to meet changing times
by passing legislation.

00:36:23.210 --> 00:36:26.660
The Constitution
isn't the panacea that

00:36:26.660 --> 00:36:28.230
cures every societal problem.

00:36:28.230 --> 00:36:33.067
We have to trust our
political branches to do that.

00:36:33.067 --> 00:36:34.650
STEPHANIE MALONEY:
One final question.

00:36:34.650 --> 00:36:39.770
On a personal note, you are
a wife, mother of seven,

00:36:39.770 --> 00:36:42.850
a law professor,
and a federal judge.

00:36:42.850 --> 00:36:45.360
So can women really have it all?

00:36:45.360 --> 00:36:47.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:47.820 --> 00:36:51.780
And if you had to give advice
to young women at Hillsdale

00:36:51.780 --> 00:36:54.810
and other universities
for pursuing

00:36:54.810 --> 00:36:57.406
a career in the law, what
would that look like?

00:36:57.406 --> 00:37:02.610
AMY CONEY BARRETT:
I think each woman

00:37:02.610 --> 00:37:04.350
is called to a unique path.

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:06.570
I don't think there's
one cookie cutter

00:37:06.570 --> 00:37:08.430
way that women should proceed.

00:37:08.430 --> 00:37:12.720
And I didn't know exactly what
I wanted my life to look like.

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:14.550
It kind of became a
work-- it was always,

00:37:14.550 --> 00:37:17.830
it still is a work in progress.

00:37:17.830 --> 00:37:19.770
We will celebrate
the 100th anniversary

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:21.720
of the 19th Amendment
next year, which

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:23.500
gave women the right to vote.

00:37:23.500 --> 00:37:28.140
And I think that we should
celebrate the ability of women

00:37:28.140 --> 00:37:32.370
to not only cast their
ballots, but to participate

00:37:32.370 --> 00:37:35.880
in both the private and the
public spheres in so many ways

00:37:35.880 --> 00:37:37.890
that our grandmothers
were not able to.

00:37:37.890 --> 00:37:39.420
So yes.

00:37:39.420 --> 00:37:42.170
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:43.658 --> 00:37:48.820
But one thing I
often think, when--

00:37:48.820 --> 00:37:51.690
I'll get asked this
question a lot,

00:37:51.690 --> 00:37:53.520
as I think most
working mothers do,

00:37:53.520 --> 00:37:55.830
about balancing
career and family.

00:37:55.830 --> 00:37:58.950
One thing I think that is really
important not to lose sight of

00:37:58.950 --> 00:38:02.220
is that we are so
fortunate, as women living

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:06.000
in 2019, to have so many more
doors open to us and so many

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:07.480
choices.

00:38:07.480 --> 00:38:09.820
But I think as part
of that conversation,

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:11.250
I always want to
be careful never

00:38:11.250 --> 00:38:16.740
to devalue the choices that
women make that are different.

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:19.830
Women shouldn't have
to be cookie cutters.

00:38:19.830 --> 00:38:22.320
So some women have
the flexibility

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:24.720
to be able to stay
home with children.

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:26.820
I have seven
children and I'm also

00:38:26.820 --> 00:38:28.680
the oldest of seven children.

00:38:28.680 --> 00:38:30.930
And I have five sisters.

00:38:30.930 --> 00:38:34.638
And three of us work full-time,
two of us work part-time,

00:38:34.638 --> 00:38:36.180
and one stays home
with her children.

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:37.710
And she homeschools them.

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:41.290
And she probably works
harder than the rest of us.

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:45.060
So I think we should
celebrate and try

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:48.750
to make possible and support
and think creatively about ways

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:52.750
to provide childcare for women
who are in the workplace.

00:38:52.750 --> 00:38:55.740
And then I think we should
also celebrate the choices,

00:38:55.740 --> 00:38:58.350
like my sister, Eileen, who
is home with her children,

00:38:58.350 --> 00:39:02.880
and my own mother, make to
stay home with their children.

00:39:02.880 --> 00:39:06.900
I just think that one
freedom that we should really

00:39:06.900 --> 00:39:10.980
celebrate as women is that
we don't have to just follow

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:13.680
a path that seems like the
one that people tell us

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:14.710
that we have to follow.

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:17.190
And we don't just have to
think what people tell us

00:39:17.190 --> 00:39:20.983
that we might have to think as
women, that we're really free.

00:39:20.983 --> 00:39:22.650
And that if you want
to live a life that

00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:26.550
looks a little bit different,
I mean, you know, my friend--

00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:28.650
I have seven children.

00:39:28.650 --> 00:39:30.150
Two of them are
adopted from Haiti

00:39:30.150 --> 00:39:32.500
and our youngest
has Downs syndrome.

00:39:32.500 --> 00:39:36.030
And when my son,
Benjamin, was born

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:38.970
and he was in the NICU,
my friend, my dear

00:39:38.970 --> 00:39:42.960
friend who I've known for a
long time, was sitting with me--

00:39:42.960 --> 00:39:46.230
it was a very difficult
time, he was on oxygen--

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:47.730
just holding my hand.

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:50.610
She looked up at me
and she said, do you

00:39:50.610 --> 00:39:51.780
have to be so competitive?

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:54.660
You already had the most
interesting Christmas card

00:39:54.660 --> 00:39:55.550
on the mantle.

00:39:55.550 --> 00:39:58.536
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:59.036 --> 00:40:01.500
We can make choices
to have a life that

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:03.815
looks different or odd.

00:40:03.815 --> 00:40:06.660
I mean, you can be
free to be different.

00:40:06.660 --> 00:40:09.930
And you can stay home with
children or you can work.

00:40:09.930 --> 00:40:12.090
But you should
respect the choices

00:40:12.090 --> 00:40:15.450
that all women make in that
regard and the contributions

00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:18.560
that all women contribute.

00:40:18.560 --> 00:40:21.970
STEPHANIE MALONEY: Do you have
any final closing thoughts?

00:40:21.970 --> 00:40:23.720
AMY CONEY BARRETT:
Gosh, I don't think so.

00:40:23.720 --> 00:40:25.810
I'm just so grateful that
you all came tonight.

00:40:25.810 --> 00:40:28.114
And it was wonderful
to be with you.

00:40:28.114 --> 00:40:30.102
AUDIENCE: Thank you
for [INAUDIBLE]..

00:40:30.102 --> 00:40:33.581
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:42.030 --> 00:40:43.050
MATTHEW SPALDING: OK.

00:40:43.050 --> 00:40:43.758
Thank you, Judge.

00:40:43.758 --> 00:40:45.633
And thank you, Stephanie,
for that interview.

00:40:45.633 --> 00:40:46.730
That was wonderful.

00:40:46.730 --> 00:40:48.210
I have two things for you.

00:40:48.210 --> 00:40:51.770
One is, it's not Notre Dame
green, but it's Hillsdale blue.

00:40:55.090 --> 00:40:56.436
AMY CONEY BARRETT: Thank you.

00:40:56.436 --> 00:40:57.310
I'll treasure it.

00:40:57.310 --> 00:41:00.116
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:01.373 --> 00:41:02.790
MATTHEW SPALDING:
And of course, I

00:41:02.790 --> 00:41:05.100
do have a last final, final
question for you, which

00:41:05.100 --> 00:41:07.340
is, what is your position
about Hillsdale students

00:41:07.340 --> 00:41:08.750
as law clerks, and--

00:41:08.750 --> 00:41:10.682
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:10.682 --> 00:41:12.140
AMY CONEY BARRETT:
Send them to me.

00:41:12.140 --> 00:41:13.320
MATTHEW SPALDING: Thank you.

00:41:13.320 --> 00:41:14.770
And thank all those--

00:41:14.770 --> 00:41:17.100
a lot of people are
watching our live streaming,

00:41:17.100 --> 00:41:18.420
both on campus and elsewhere.

00:41:18.420 --> 00:41:19.420
I thank them for joining us.

00:41:19.420 --> 00:41:20.560
Thank you all for joining us.

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:21.480
And have a wonderful evening.

00:41:21.480 --> 00:41:22.210
Thank you very much.

00:41:22.210 --> 00:41:23.418
AMY CONEY BARRETT: Thank you.

00:41:23.418 --> 00:41:25.860
[APPLAUSE]

